ukraine president volodomir zielinski  had a zoom call with more than 300 u.s  lawmakers pleading for help in getting  nato to declare a no-fly zone over  ukraine nato is refusing saying it could  lead to a full-fledged war in europe  on saturday russia president vladimir  putin warned that could be seen by  moscow as a step into the military  conflict  nato is also refusing to supply ukraine  with fighter jets lawmakers promised to  push u.s president joe biden to make  that happen there are planes available  by nato nations migs that the ukrainians  can fly  they're waiting to be delivered and  apparently the united states  is part of the problem not the solution  zielinski is also asking the u.s to stop  buying russian oil which amounts to  about three to four percent of american  consumption guys like putin they don't  stop  and we got to recognize that the white  house and congress are now pledging  another 10 billion dollars in  humanitarian aid as ukrainians leave  their homeland more than 700 000 people  have been forced to flee the violence  perpetrated by russia by crossing the  border from ukraine to poland  secretary of state blinken visited a  refugee center in poland as that  country's leaders warned putin there  will be consequences for his actions  shelling residential areas  nuclear power plants are war crimes  under international law  which are not subjected to statue of  limitation the u.s state department and  the canadian government are now advising  citizens to leave russia immediately  this came as news emerged that russia  arrested wnba star brittany griner last  month charging her with drug smuggling  for allegedly having vape cartridges  containing hashish oil in her carry-on  luggage this video reportedly showing  the search was released by russia  customs service the problem is that  there is no reliable independent  judiciary in russia  so consequently the expectation of a  fair trial  is not there griner plays for a russian  team during the wnba off-season if  convicted she could spend up to 10 years  in prison her agent says they are  working on getting her back to the u.s  jennifer johnson global news washington
 

